From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Warren <816217+joshuaswarren@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:42:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: vote GPU activity to the PMP so memory leaves
 idle DVFS on t600x

On the M1 Max (t6001) the PMP keeps memory at its idle floor for a busy
GPU until the AGX power request, bit 9 of the PMP report's PS-REQ word, is
set. Nothing sets it on Linux, so GPU reads top out at 241 GB/s. The same
chip reads 361 GB/s on macOS.

Report GPU activity to the PMP. The GPU driver calls
apple_pmp_agx_activity_begin() when a submission starts and
apple_pmp_agx_activity_end() when it finishes. pmp-report sets bit 9 when
the first operation starts and clears it 100 ms after the last one ends.
It also clears it at once when the GPU driver unbinds or the report goes
away, so the request is never left set past the GPU's power-domain
release. The register sequence is the one apple_pmp_report_set_state()
already uses for report entries: read 0xf80, write 0x107c0, poll 0x1000,
status 0x10, 50 ms acknowledge. A set that is not acknowledged is cleared
again and not retried for a second.

The vote is off by default. pmp_report.agx_vote=1 turns it on, and
pmp_report.agx_vote_idle_ms (default 100) sets the hold. With it off
nothing is written. It applies to apple,t6001 only; t6000 is untested.

The decisions (set, clear, hold, retry, wrap of the 32-bit millisecond
clock) are plain functions in pmp-agx-vote.h with no kernel dependency.

Setting the bit by hand with a test module on a 16-inch M1 Max moved a GPU
read microbenchmark from 241 to 368 GB/s. This patch has not yet been run
on hardware.

Signed-off-by: Joshua Warren <816217+joshuaswarren@users.noreply.github.com>
[j604: context-only conflict in rust/bindings/bindings_helper.h: a15y adds
 linux/suspend.h after linux/soc/apple/pmp.h; both includes kept]
(cherry picked from commit 689f2f91cc94da196482d1717a89604c6fac14c2)

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
